EXT. STREETS - DAY

A small town in the United States. Red brick buildings line the sidewalks. Cars roll down the asphalt in a steady, predictable flow.

The traffic lights glow a solid, comforting green.

INT. AANYA'S ROOM - DAY

Mustard yellow and slate blue paint covers the walls.

AANYA (8) stands before a giant, hand-drawn map of the city's traffic grid. She is South Asian. Her straight black hair is tied in a neat, low plait. She wears a mustard yellow jumper with the sleeves pulled over her hands, and grey leggings.

Her eyes trace the lines on the map. Each intersection has a number written next to it: 12, 12, 12.

She taps her chin. She loves these numbers. They are the seconds the green lights stay active. A perfect, predictable rhythm.

Aanya reaches onto her desk. She picks up her totem: a smooth grey pebble. She rolls it between her fingers. It feels cool and solid.

She slides the pebble into her pocket.

Aanya grabs her notebook and a blue pen. She loops a pair of grey noise-reducing headphones around her neck.

She looks at the map. At the very edge of the grid is a green circle labeled: THE GLASS DOME CONSERVATORY.

Aanya speaks to herself. Her voice is calm, clear, and steady.

AANYA
Twelve seconds of green at Oak. Twelve seconds at Maple. If I walk at my steady pace, I will reach the conservatory without stopping once. The quiet room awaits.

She smiles. She wants this quiet walk more than anything today.

EXT. OAK STREET - DAY

Aanya steps onto the sidewalk. The autumn air is crisp.

She keeps her hands tucked inside her mustard yellow sleeves. In her pocket, her thumb turns the smooth grey pebble.

She walks with a measured stride. One step per second.

She approaches the corner of Oak Street. The pedestrian light counts down: 3, 2, 1.

But the main traffic light does not turn red. It flashes yellow.

Aanya stops. Her brow furrows.

The light blinks. 

Blink. Blink. Blink. (Pause)

Blink. (Pause)

Blink. Blink. Blink. Blink. (Pause)

Blink. Blink.

Aanya opens her notebook. She writes down the numbers of the flashes: 3, 1, 4, 2.

AANYA
Three. One. Four. Two. That is not the standard maintenance flash.

A car behind the line honks its horn. The sharp sound makes Aanya flinch. She quickly pulls her noise-reducing headphones over her ears.

The loud world softens. The honk becomes a dull thud. She keeps her eyes fixed on the blinking light.

EXT. MAPLE AVENUE - DAY

Aanya walks quickly to the next block. Her eyes scan the overhead signals.

At Maple Avenue, the light is doing the exact same thing.

Blink-blink-blink. Blink. Blink-blink-blink-blink. Blink-blink.

But it is faster. Much faster.

Aanya pulls out her pen. She times the sequence with her breath. Her internal clock is highly precise.

AANYA
The pattern is the same. Three, one, four, two. But the pause between the sequences has shrunk from six seconds to three seconds.

She looks down the street. The next light at Pine Street starts to blink in the same pattern.

It is a chain reaction. A countdown.

EXT. MAPLE AVENUE - CONTINUOUS

OFFICER HIGGINS (50s) stands near the crosswalk. He wears a bright orange vest. He looks flustered. He waves his hands at a line of stopped cars.

OFFICER HIGGINS
Keep moving! Just a minor glitch! Keep it moving!

Aanya steps up to him. She lowers her headphones to her neck.

AANYA
Officer Higgins. The lights are not experiencing a minor glitch. They are synchronizing to a feedback loop.

Officer Higgins looks down, surprised.

OFFICER HIGGINS
What's that, kiddo? I can't hear you over the engines.

AANYA
I am autistic. I do not guess. I notice patterns. The lights are flashing the sequence three-one-four-two. The interval is shrinking by one second every ninety seconds. In exactly three minutes, the interval will hit zero.

Officer Higgins chuckles nervously, waving another car forward.

OFFICER HIGGINS
Thanks, budget-Einstein. But the city computer runs all this. It's probably just a dusty bulb. Run along to the park.

AANYA
When the interval hits zero, the central server will lock up. Every light in the city will turn red. The noise will be unbearable.

Officer Higgins sighs and turns back to the traffic. He does not believe her.

OFFICER HIGGINS
Just let the grown-ups handle it, okay?

Aanya looks at her map, then at her notebook. She knows the rules of the grid. She knows she is right.

She looks toward the Glass Dome Conservatory. It is only three blocks away. She could run there. She could hide in the quiet.

She feels the smooth grey pebble in her pocket. She turns it twice.

AANYA
If the grid locks, the emergency vehicles cannot pass. The city will be stuck. I have to go to the control room.

She turns away from her quiet destination. She chooses to run toward the municipal control building.

EXT. MUNICIPAL CONTROL BUILDING - DAY

A modern concrete building with large windows. 

Outside, the traffic has ground to a halt. Horns blare from every direction. The sound is a chaotic wall of noise.

Aanya presses her headphones tightly against her ears. She pushes open the heavy glass door of the building.

INT. MUNICIPAL CONTROL ROOM - DAY

Inside, the atmosphere is tense.

Wall-sized screens display a digital map of the city. Dozens of intersection icons are flashing red.

SARAH JENKINS (30s) wears a blue technician's uniform. She is typing furiously on a keyboard. Her forehead is damp with sweat.

SARAH
Come on, reboot! Why won't you accept the manual override?

Aanya walks into the room. Her footsteps are silent on the carpet. She stands behind Sarah, looking at the main monitor.

On the screen, lines of code scroll by. Among the letters, Aanya's eyes spot the numbers: 3, 1, 4, 2.

They are repeating in a tight, endless column.

AANYA
The timing buffer is full.

Sarah jumps, spinning around in her chair.

SARAH
Whoa! Who let you in here? This is a restricted area, kid!

AANYA
My name is Aanya. The system is rejecting your reboot because the feedback loop is still active in the signal cache. You are trying to clear the screen, but you are not clearing the memory.

Sarah blinks, looking from Aanya to the screen.

SARAH
How do you know what a signal cache is?

AANYA
I study the grid. I map the sequences. The lights outside are flashing three-one-four-two. It is a system loop.

Sarah looks at the scrolling code. Her eyes widen as she spots the pattern Aanya pointed out.

SARAH
Oh my gosh. You're right. The master controller is stuck in a self-diagnostic loop. But the keyboard is completely locked out. I can't type the override code.

AANYA
We must use the manual bypass panel.

Sarah points to a heavy metal box on the side wall. It has a glass door and four large mechanical levers labeled A, B, C, and D.

SARAH
I tried that! I pulled them all down, but nothing happened. The mechanical relays didn't register.

Aanya walks over to the metal box. She opens the glass door. She looks at the levers. 

She touches her grey pebble through her pocket. Her mind clears. She sees the rhythm.

AANYA
You pulled them all at once. The system needs the inverse sequence to break the loop. And it must be done in the exact timing of the blink.

SARAH
The inverse? You mean two-four-one-three?

AANYA
Yes. But we must pull them on the beat. If we are off by even a millisecond, the buffer will reset and lock the grid permanently.

Sarah looks at the screen. A digital countdown timer reads: 00:18.

SARAH
I don't know the timing! The blink rate is changing too fast!

AANYA
I know the timing. I can feel the rhythm.

Aanya steps up to the levers. She takes a deep breath. She pulls her hands out of her mustard yellow sleeves.

She places her fingers on the cold metal handles.

SARAH
Are you sure about this, Aanya?

AANYA
Yes. I am autistic. My brain does not ignore the rhythm. I am ready.

The digital countdown hits: 00:10.

Outside, the chorus of car horns reaches a painful crescendo. The building seems to vibrate.

Aanya closes her eyes. She blocks out the noise. She focuses entirely on the rhythm of the flashing red light on the panel.

Blink. (Pause) Blink.

She counts the milliseconds in her head. She tracks the shrinking gap.

Now.

She pulls Lever B (2).

Click.

She waits exactly 0.4 seconds.

She pulls Lever D (4).

Click.

She waits exactly 0.1 seconds.

She pulls Lever A (1).

Click.

She waits exactly 0.2 seconds.

She pulls Lever C (3).

Click.

The final lever locks into place.

For a second, the control room is dead silent.

Then, a soft, musical chime echoes from the console.

The flashing red lights on the wall map turn a solid, peaceful green.

Sarah holds her breath, looking at the screens.

SARAH
System restored. The cache is clear. Aanya, you did it!

Sarah laughs and claps her hands. 

Outside, the sound of blaring horns begins to fade. The cars start to roll forward, their engines humming in a gentle, steady line.

Aanya lets go of the levers. She tucks her hands back into her mustard yellow jumper sleeves.

AANYA
The green wave is restored. Twelve seconds per intersection.

SARAH
I've been trying to fix that loop for an hour. How did you see it so quickly?

AANYA
Some people see a glitch. I see the pattern. It is how my mind works.

Sarah smiles warmly, looking at Aanya with immense respect.

SARAH
Well, your mind just saved this city a massive headache. Thank you, Aanya.

Aanya nods. She pulls her noise-reducing headphones down around her neck.

AANYA
You are welcome. Now, I must go. My walk is behind schedule.

EXT. STREETS - DAY

Aanya walks down the sidewalk.

The traffic lights change from red to green in perfect, twelve-second intervals.

She steps in time with the signals. Her pace is light and happy.

EXT. CONSERVATORY - DAY

At the end of the street stands the Glass Dome Conservatory. Sunlight glints off its curved glass panels.

Aanya pushes open the door.

INT. CONSERVATORY - CONTINUOUS

The air inside is warm and damp. 

Huge green ferns hang from the ceiling. A small stone waterfall trickles into a clear pool.

It is completely quiet.

Aanya walks to a wooden bench nestled under a giant palm leaf.

She sits down. She opens her notebook to a clean page.

She reaches into her pocket. She pulls out her smooth grey pebble and places it on the bench beside her.

She is autistic, she is quiet, and her world is perfectly in rhythm.

She begins to read. 

FADE OUT.